        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0016", "runtime", Severity.Error, "exit code out of bounds: {code} ({range} expected)", "`process.exit_with_code(n)` requires `n` ∈ 0..255 and `process.exit_with_message(n, …)` requires `n` ∈ 1..255, since a failure message cannot announce a success (250–255 are also scripts's own failure codes; 129/130/143 are signal codes and 141 is EPIPE)", "use a code in {range}", "the start of the current statement", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0017", "runtime", Severity.Error, "unhandled error: {error_description}", "an `error(x)` reached the top level through `?`; the description is « kind : subject : message » of the same unified error `error{kind,message,subject}` (or « kind : message » when `subject` is empty, e.g. `not_encodable : this value has no JSON form`), or the text of the error (`string`)", "handle the error with `match`, or convert it with `.transform_error(…)` and handle it higher up", "the start of the statement that contains the `?`", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0018", "runtime", Severity.Error, "interrupted by {signal} (exit code {exit_code})", "scripts received `SIGINT` (130), `SIGTERM` (143) or `SIGHUP` (129); a `SIGINT` or `SIGHUP` ignored when `scripts` started (a background job, `nohup`) stays ignored, per POSIX convention; the current child and its still-attached descendants receive `SIGTERM` then `SIGKILL` after 2 s — a `/proc` walk deepest-first, then `killpg` on the child's own group for a captured child; `process.run_inherit` keeps scripts's group and is signalled with its `/proc`-visible descendants (S18). Stdout is flushed; a 5 s watchdog forces exit if a blocking call does not return, replaced by a 30 s backstop during the deferred cleanup", "rerun the script; a script cannot intercept signals", "the start of the current statement at the moment of the signal", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0019", "runtime", Severity.Error, "write failure on {stream}: {reason}", "a write to stdout or stderr fails otherwise than by `EPIPE` on stdout (which terminates silently with code 141); an `EPIPE` on stderr is treated as this failure, with no displayable diagnostic", "check the destination of the redirection (disk space, valid descriptor)", "the start of the statement that triggered the flush; `null` if the failure appears only at the final flush", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0020", "runtime", Severity.Error, "evaluation failure of the constant `{name}`: {cause_message}", "the constants of a file are evaluated before the first statement; an evaluation error (division by zero, overflow, non-finite float, string too long, a path join whose computed right operand is absolute or empty) is reported instead of the underlying execution code, before any output", "fix the constant expression `{name}` (values or operators)", "the start of the constant declaration", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0021", "runtime", Severity.Error, "assertion failed: {message}", "`assert(condition, message)` received `false`: the invariant the script states does not hold, so the run stops (code 253) instead of continuing with a corrupted state. An expected failure is a `result`, not an assertion.", "fix the invariant or the value that violates it; use a `result`/`?` for an expected failure", "the `assert` call", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-RUNTIME-0022", "runtime", Severity.Error, "the right operand of `/` on a path is `{value}`: it is absolute or empty", "`a / b` joins paths lexically and fails closed: a computed operand that starts with `/` would silently replace the base path, and an empty one has no segment to add. The value is known only at run time, so the join aborts instead of guessing.", "check the operand before joining (strip the leading `/`, or reject an empty segment) and build it with `text.to_path()`", "the start of the current statement", "active"),
