        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0005", "lint", Severity.Warning, "`with_standard_input` receives {argument}, which looks like a working directory", "the argument of `run_request.with_standard_input` is a literal string starting with `/`, `./` or `../`, so a directory was probably meant: the working directory is set by `with_working_directory`, and the child would be fed the path text as its standard input instead of reading from that directory", "use `with_working_directory(<directory_path>)` for the directory and keep the standard input to the text the child should read", "the argument of `with_standard_input`", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0006", "lint", Severity.Warning, "`{name}` holds a process outcome that is never consumed", "a binding that holds a `process_result`, or the `result<integer, error>` of `process.run_inherit`, is never read at all (the narrower stdout-only shape needs data flow and stays deferred,): the child may have exited non-zero and nothing in the script looks at `exit_code` or calls `require_success`, so the failure is silently ignored. The rule is deliberately narrow: it inspects the binding, not the data flow, so a value passed to a function, stored in a list or returned is not flagged (the deferred data-flow warning of stays deferred)", "read the outcome (`result.exit_code`), turn it into a failure with `result.require_success()?`, or discard it with `let _ = …` only once the failure is known to be impossible", "the name of the binding", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0007", "lint", Severity.Warning, "the main script declares `{name}` but never calls it: nothing runs", "the main script (not a module) declares a function named `main` and no call resolves to it. A script is a sequence of statements: `main` is not an entry point and is never called implicitly, so the script runs its other statements (or nothing) and exits 0, which looks like a silent no-op. The rule fires on the name `main` only, because that is the name a newcomer brings from C, Java, Go or Rust; any other unused function is ordinary dead code, not a trap", "call it at the end of the script (`main()` when it returns `unit`, `main()?` when it returns a `result`), or rename it and keep the statements at the top level", "the name of the declaration", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0008", "lint", Severity.Warning, "`{name}` holds a fallible value that is discarded", "a binding whose type contains `result`, named `_` or starting with `_`, whose initializer was not propagated with `?`: the `_` exemption applies to the `SCRIPTS-TYPE` check, not to the failure, so the error is erased", "propagate with `?`, handle with `match`, or name the default with `value_or`", "the name of the binding", "retired"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0009", "lint", Severity.Warning, "the right operand of `/` is {value}, so the base path is ignored", "a literal path segment that starts with `/` replaces the base path (`root / \"/etc/passwd\"` is `/etc/passwd`); a literal `\"\"` addresses the base itself (`root / \"\"` is `root`). Literal-only, like `SCRIPTS-LINT-0005`, so a computed segment is never flagged", "drop the leading `/`, or build the join from a checked segment (`text.to_path()`)", "the literal", "retired"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0010", "lint", Severity.Warning, "the process handle `{name}` is never waited for or killed", "a `let` binding of type `process_handle` is never passed to `process.wait_for_process` or `process.terminate`: the child keeps running until scripts exits, and a long script accumulates it", "call `process.wait_for_process(handle, timeout)`, or `process.terminate(handle)`", "the name of the binding", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0011", "lint", Severity.Warning, "the walk report `{name}` is never checked for skipped entries", "`filesystem.walk_directory` returns partial results instead of aborting: an unreadable directory or an undecodable name lands in `.skipped_paths`, so a script that reads only `.entries` silently loses a subtree", "read `.skipped_paths` and report or handle it", "the name of the binding", "active"),
        new("SCRIPTS-LINT-0012", "lint", Severity.Warning, "string self-concatenation in a loop is quadratic; accumulate a `list<string>` and `join(\"\")`", "a mutable `string` is assigned from itself with `+` (`s = s + …`) or `+=` (`s += …`) directly in a `for`/`while` body, including an `if` or `match` arm inside it; a mutable `string` declared inside the loop body is rebuilt per iteration and is not reported, and a lambda body is not analysed: each concatenation copies the accumulated string, so the total work grows quadratically with the accumulated length", "accumulate the pieces in a `list<string>` and build the result once with `join(\"\")`", "the assignment target", "active"),
